From 62aa4d17730c6ae92c806f038394b1ba91275df7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tianyu9748 <54686548+Tianyu9748@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 02:02:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 23/26] [Diffusion] Workload timer: opt-in request marks

runtime/utils/workload_timer.py (new): WorkloadTimer with mark(name), a
span(name) context manager and resolve() -> {"marks": {name: {host_ns,
gpu_ms_from_t1}}, "spans": [{name, host_start_ns, host_end_ns, gpu_ms}]}.
Host times are time.monotonic_ns(); GPU times are CUDA events with
enable_timing on the current stream, read only by resolve() after the
output exists. A name is recorded once (first occurrence).

GPUWorker.execute_forward attaches a timer as req.extra["wl_timer"] when
the request asks for it (req.extra["workload_timing"] is True) or
SGLANG_WL_TIMER=1, marks t1_dispatch at entry (single-request batches)
and stores the resolved dict as req.extra["wl_timing"] after the forward.
rgb_ready is marked once return_frames has materialized the output as
uint8 [F, H, W, 3] CPU frames (the existing CPU uint8 path). The decoding
stage marks vae_begin / vae_end around the VAE decode (context and new
latents of output="new"). The causal denoising stage marks
t2_first_new_denoise in _forward_causal_transformer immediately before
the first forward that is not a clean forward: every clean (context,
recache, warm-up) forward enters _causal_kv_capture_scope, which now
counts them, so restored blocks, a LongLive 1.0 recache or a replayed
history never set it.

Requests without a timer record nothing and run the same code paths;
outputs are unchanged. New unit test test/unit/test_wl_timer.py.
